# This is `bat`s configuration file. Each line either contains a comment or
# a command-line option that you want to pass to `bat` by default. You can
# run `bat --help` to get a list of all possible configuration options.

# Specify desired highlighting theme (e.g. "TwoDark"). Run `bat --list-themes`
# for a list of all available themes
# --theme="TwoDark"
# --theme="gruvbox-dark"
--theme="Catppuccin Mocha"

# Enable this to use italic text on the terminal. This is not supported on all
# terminal emulators (like tmux, by default):
--italic-text=always

# Uncomment the following line to disable automatic paging:
#--paging=never
--paging=always

# Pager selection.
#
# IMPORTANT: do NOT hard-code `less` here. bat's config is a static file with no
# conditionals, and when the configured pager binary is MISSING bat does not
# error - it silently gives up on paging and dumps the whole input straight to
# the prompt. That was the "pager does not page" bug: `less` was not installed.
#
# `builtin` is bat's own bundled pager (minus). It needs no external binary and
# is always available, so paging keeps working on any machine.
#
# LIMITATION: minus draws a single, HARD-CODED prompt string ("bat") at the
# bottom. It has no position/progress indicator - no percentage, no "lines
# x-y of N". The prompt text is set by bat via minus's set_prompt() API and
# there is no env var or flag to change it. Install `less` to get a real
# position + percentage prompt (see below).
--pager="builtin"

# If `less` IS installed and you prefer it, use this instead (it gives you
# search, --mouse scrolling, --quit-if-one-screen and a POSITION/PERCENT
# prompt via -P):
# --pager="less --RAW-CONTROL-CHARS --quit-if-one-screen --mouse"
#
# With less you can also enable its status column, which is the closest thing
# to a progress indicator a pager offers:
#   less -R -+F -P 'lines %lt-%lb of %L (%pB)'
# (bat accepts only a plain command here, so put flags in $LESS instead if the
#  quoting gets awkward - see the LESS variable in shell/env.sh.)

# Syntax mappings: map a certain filename pattern to a language.
#   Example 1: use the C++ syntax for .ino files
#   Example 2: Use ".gitignore"-style highlighting for ".ignore" files
#--map-syntax "*.ino:C++"
#--map-syntax ".ignore:Git Ignore"
# Handy extras worth enabling on this machine:
#   *.conf/*.toml are already autodetected; these are for odd extensions.
# NOTE: this bat build has no "Makefile" syntax, so Justfile cannot be mapped
# to it. Leave Justfile to the shell/Debian syntax bat picks by default.
#--map-syntax "*.tera:HTML"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Decorations / style
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Components available in bat 0.26.1:
#   changes, grid, header-filename, header-filesize, numbers, rule, snip
#
# `header-filesize` adds the file size to the header (only shown when bat gets
# one or more FILE arguments - not for piped stdin). It is the closest thing to
# extra "info" bat can display, since the pager owns the bottom status line.
#
# Use `--style=full` for everything, or `-p`/BAT_STYLE=plain for none.
--style="header-filename,header-filesize,changes,grid"

# Tabs: bat's default expands a tab to 4 spaces. `--tabs=0` passes tabs through
# unchanged (useful when copying out of the pager).
#--tabs=0

# Wrap long lines. `never` truncates instead of wrapping, which keeps the grid
# aligned but hides long lines - handy for wide CSV/log files.
#--wrap=never

# Neutralize a possible BAT_STYLE/BAT_THEME exported by another tool. Options
# here are defaults only; the command line and BAT_* env vars still win.
#--theme-dark="Catppuccin Mocha"
#--theme-light="Catppuccin Latte"
